    <dashboard name='Story 1' type='storyboard'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run>Linear trends and mileage</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
      </layout-options>
      <style>
        <style-rule element='story-point-caption'>
          <format attr='width' value='691' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='964' maxwidth='1016' minheight='964' minwidth='1016' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98340' id='1' param='vert' removable='false' type-v2='layout-flow' w='98426' x='787' y='830'>
            <zone h='3423' id='3' type-v2='title' w='98426' x='787' y='830' />
            <zone fixed-size='84' h='8714' id='4' is-fixed='true' paired-zone-id='5' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard-nav' w='98426' x='787' y='4253' />
            <zone h='86203' id='5' paired-zone-id='4' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard' w='98426' x='787' y='12967'>
              <flipboard active-id='2' nav-type='caption' show-nav-arrows='true'>
                <story-points>
                  <story-point caption='The present dataset contains a large number of correlated numerical features. While manufacturer seems to be the best linear predictor for price, engine features such as mechanics and power, as well as car dimensions and mileage also show strong positive or negative trends. When assessing relationships with price, Citympg is preferred to Highwaympg due to its stronger and clearer negative linear trend.' captured-sheet='Sheet 1' id='1' />
                  <story-point caption='Here you can see the downward trendlines between city and highway miles per galon - while these seem almost parallel, the linear trend is stronger for citympg than highway mpg (making it a more suitable choice for linear reggression). In fact, both trends may be interptreted as hyperbolic, or may be fitted with polynomials of degrees 3 and 4, respectively - but this kind of variability may warn of overfitting.' captured-sheet='Sheet 3' id='2' />
                </story-points>
              </flipboard>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone flipboard-zone-id='5' h='5602' id='6' story-point-id='2' type-v2='text' w='33268' x='48524' y='11100'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontalignment='0' fontsize='10'>By ticking manufacturer boxes, the viewer can check for themselves the price concentration of each manufacturer, in each scatterplot.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{016CF8B3-1A77-467E-B635-F94F175BF00E}' />
    </dashboard>
  </dashboards>
